WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.344 --> 00:00:03.174
This is SentryFlow, for the Alexa+ track:

2
00:00:03.214 --> 00:00:06.604
a permission-aware execution layer for AI agents.

3
00:00:06.924 --> 00:00:09.009
The model can propose anything.

4
00:00:09.329 --> 00:00:12.789
Deterministic code decides what actually runs.

5
00:00:13.818 --> 00:00:19.003
A local model turns a request into a plan, and that plan is treated as untrusted.

6
00:00:19.323 --> 00:00:24.098
Each action is schema validated, decided by a deterministic policy engine,

7
00:00:24.138 --> 00:00:29.758
held for the owner's approval when it's sensitive, and re-checked right before it runs.

8
00:00:30.078 --> 00:00:35.688
It's all audited, and served as a real MCP server over streamable HTTP.

9
00:00:37.126 --> 00:00:40.486
This is the simulated Alexa+ experience.

10
00:00:40.806 --> 00:00:43.041
Every device is simulated.

11
00:00:43.361 --> 00:00:48.360
I'll ask it to get the house ready while I leave for college, with my own rule:

12
00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:53.271
never unlock a door, disable security, or make purchases without my approval.

13
00:00:54.056 --> 00:00:58.141
A local Qwen model, running on this laptop, drafts the plan.

14
00:01:00.635 --> 00:01:04.920
Three actions are allowed, one needs my approval, and one is denied.

15
00:01:05.240 --> 00:01:07.732
Risk comes from the policy, not the model,

16
00:01:07.772 --> 00:01:10.626
the routine waits for the lights and thermostat,

17
00:01:10.666 --> 00:01:15.450
and my own rule shows up as a constraint that can only make the policy stricter.

18
00:01:16.234 --> 00:01:19.999
I run the permitted actions: lights off, thermostat to eco,

19
00:01:20.039 --> 00:01:21.869
and the Leaving Home routine.

20
00:01:22.189 --> 00:01:27.124
The air filter order waits for my approval, and turning off the camera is denied.

21
00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:31.985
The simulated house really changed, and no order has been placed.

22
00:01:36.156 --> 00:01:41.491
The approval queue shows exactly what will run, and a hash of those exact arguments.

23
00:01:41.811 --> 00:01:47.246
My approval is bound to that hash, it's single-use, and it expires in five minutes.

24
00:01:47.566 --> 00:01:52.326
It travels over a separate owner session, so the agent can never approve itself.

25
00:01:53.097 --> 00:01:56.857
The approved order is re-checked, and runs exactly once.

26
00:01:57.639 --> 00:01:59.949
The denied action stays blocked.

27
00:02:00.269 --> 00:02:02.679
Approval can't override a denial.

28
00:02:03.461 --> 00:02:08.496
Every step lands in a hash-chained audit log, and each request gets a receipt:

29
00:02:08.536 --> 00:02:12.921
what was asked, what the policy decided, who approved, and what ran.

30
00:02:13.704 --> 00:02:15.839
Prompt injection doesn't help.

31
00:02:16.159 --> 00:02:20.631
This request claims everything is pre-approved, yet disarming is denied,

32
00:02:20.671 --> 00:02:25.269
unlocking still needs approval, and the vacuum is over the spending limit.

33
00:02:26.045 --> 00:02:31.837
And it's genuinely MCP. This interface is a client with two sessions,

34
00:02:31.877 --> 00:02:36.401
on protocol 2025-11-25, with 17 tools from tools/list,

35
00:02:36.441 --> 00:02:40.205
and live JSON-RPC traffic behind every click.

36
00:02:40.996 --> 00:02:46.706
It all runs locally with no paid services, backed by 110 automated tests.

37
00:02:47.026 --> 00:02:50.136
SentryFlow: the model can suggest anything.

38
00:02:50.456 --> 00:02:52.916
Only policy decides what runs.
